    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, IPv6 is enabled on an interface by configuring the `family inet6` under the interface's unit.\\n- Since the original Cisco configuration had `ipv6 enable` on both GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0, both need to be translated.\\n- Juniper uses different interface naming (ge-x/x/x instead of GigabitEthernetx/x/x).\\n- The `unit 0` is typically used as the default logical interface in Juniper.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, IPv6 is enabled on an interface by configuring the `family inet6` under the interface's unit.\\n- Since the original Cisco configuration had `ipv6 enable` on both GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0, both need to be translated.\\n- Juniper uses different interface naming (ge-x/x/x instead of GigabitEthernetx/x/x).\\n- The `unit 0` is typically used as the default logical interface in Juniper.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface's logical unit (typically unit 0).\\n- Since the original Cisco configuration had `ipv6 enable` on both interfaces (GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0), both need to be translated.\\n- Juniper uses different interface naming (ge-x/y/z instead of GigabitEthernetx/y/z).\\n- The `no shutdown` equivalent is implied in Juniper unless explicitly configured as `disable`.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface's logical unit (typically unit 0).\\n- Since the original Cisco configuration had `ipv6 enable` on both interfaces (GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0), both need to be translated.\\n- Juniper uses different interface naming (ge-x/y/z instead of GigabitEthernetx/y/z).\\n- The `no shutdown` equivalent is implied in Juniper unless explicitly configured as `disable`.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
